{"name":"Pool Tide","hex":"#70928e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pool","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small and rather deep area of (usually) fresh water, as one supplied by a spring, or occurring in the course of a stream or river; a reservoir for water.","Any small body of standing or stagnant water; a puddle."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To form a pool."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pool"},{"term":"tide","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The daily fluctuation in the level of the sea caused by the gravitational influence of the moon and the sun.","The associated flow of water."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cause to float with the tide; to drive or carry with the tide or stream.","To carry over or through a problem or difficulty."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tide"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tide","blocks":[{"text":"Tides are the periodic rise and fall of sea level resulting from the differential gravitational forces exerted primarily by the Moon and the Sun, combined with inertial effects associated with the Earth–Moon system’s orbital motion and the Earth's rotation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Tide_overview.svg/330px-Tide_overview.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tide","wikidata":"Q23384"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Tide_and_Moon.jpg/330px-Tide_and_Moon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Tide_and_Moon.jpg/330px-Tide_and_Moon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"In Maine (U.S.), low tide occurs roughly at moonrise and high tide with a high Moon, corresponding to the simple gravity model of two tidal bulges; at most places however, the Moo